<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paranjothi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Siruthondar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paranjothi was a general of the Pallava king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimhavarman-i">Narasimhavarman I</a> who led the army that captured and sacked the Chalukya capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Vatapi</a> in 642. He later renounced war and became a devotee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, revered as Siruthondar, one of the 63 Nayanar saints of Tamil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a>, whose story is told in the twelfth-century Periya Puranam.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paranjothi.jpg" alt="Paranjothi" width="320" height="716" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Itsmethunder9783 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:23ty_Picture.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Paranjothi, also known as Paranjothi Mudaliar, was born into the Sengunthar Mudaliar community in the kingdom of the Pallava dynasty, whose capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>. Having mastered the art of war, he moved to Kanchipuram, then a centre of learning known across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, to study literature and Saivite scriptures. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahendravarman-i">Mahendravarman I</a>, impressed by his courage and valour, made him a commander in his army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When Mahendravarman died in 630, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimhavarman-i">Narasimhavarman I</a>, who ruled until 668, made Paranjothi his general; the two were close friends. In 642 Paranjothi led the Pallava forces to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Vatapi</a> against the Chalukya king Pulakeshin, avenging Mahendravarman's defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pullalur">Battle of Pullalur</a> in 618. Pulakeshin was killed and Vatapi was burned to the ground. The Periya Puranam tells that he led the elephant corps, razed the old city and brought back heaps of gems and riches, elephants and horses to his king.</p><p>Before the battle Paranjothi worshipped a sculpture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganesha">Ganesha</a> on the walls of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Vatapi</a> fort, and on his return he took the image to his birthplace, Tiruchenkattankudi, to be worshipped as Vatapi Ganapathi. Its shrine stands in a temple there, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagapattinam">Nagapattinam</a> district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>On the battlefield Paranjothi had a change of heart, gave up violence and devoted himself to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, becoming a wandering Saivite monk. He was then called Siruthondar, meaning small devotee, and Sekkizhar's Periya Puranam tells of his life and devotion. Several Kongu groups of the Senguntha Kaikolars or Mudaliars take as their guru Irayamangalam, a descendant of Paranjothi, whose seat is at Irayamangalam by the Kaveri in the Namakkal District. He is also a leading character of Kalki Krishnamurthy's historical novel Sivagamiyin Sapatham.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paranjothi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mahendravarman's son Narasimhavarman I, known as Mamalla, turned the tide: he defeated Pulakeshin in several battles and in 642, with his general <b>Paranjoti</b>, captured and sacked the Chalukya capital Vatapi, where Pulakeshin died, earning the title Vatapikonda, conqueror of Vatapi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Military</a></li></ul></section>
